---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Privacy"
  description="What Mixar collects, what it never collects, and how to turn it off."
  currentPage="privacy"
>
  <section class="px-section py-16 lg:py-24">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-content">
      <h1
        class="text-[clamp(2rem,5vw,2.75rem)] font-bold leading-[1.1] tracking-[-0.03em]"
      >
        Privacy
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-[60ch] text-[1.125rem] leading-relaxed text-muted">
        Mixar is open source under GPL-3.0 and you can read every line of it. This page
        describes the little data we do collect, in plain terms, so you can decide whether
        you want to share it.
      </p>

      <div class="mt-12 max-w-[68ch] space-y-10">
        <section>
          <h2 class="text-[1.5rem] font-bold tracking-tight">This website</h2>
          <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-muted">
            We use <a
              class="font-medium text-fg underline underline-offset-4 transition hover:text-accent"
              href="https://posthog.com"
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener noreferrer">PostHog</a
            > to understand which parts of this site are useful, hosted in the EU region. It runs
            in <strong class="font-semibold text-fg">cookieless mode</strong>, which means:
          </p>
          <ul class="mt-3 list-disc space-y-2 pl-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">
            <li>No cookie is set, and nothing is written to local or session storage.</li>
            <li>There is no consent banner, because there is nothing to consent to.</li>
            <li>
              You are not given a persistent identifier. PostHog counts unique visitors with a
              salted hash computed on their servers and discarded daily.
            </li>
          </ul>
          <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-muted">
            We record two things: that a page was viewed, and which outbound link was clicked
            (for example the quick-start guide, or the repository). Nothing else. There is no
            autocapture, so the text you read and the code samples on this site are never sent.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2 class="text-[1.5rem] font-bold tracking-tight">The Mixar app</h2>
          <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-muted">
            Analytics in the desktop app are <strong class="font-semibold text-fg"
              >off by default</strong
            >. On first launch Mixar asks once whether you want to send anonymous usage data.
            Decline and it never asks again; you can change your mind later in Settings.
          </p>
          <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-muted">
            If you turn it on, Mixar sends a single event when the app starts, carrying only a
            randomly generated identifier and your app version, operating system, architecture,
            and whether it is a debug or release build.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2 class="text-[1.5rem] font-bold tracking-tight">What Mixar never collects</h2>
          <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-muted">
            This is not a filter applied to your data. The component that sends analytics has no
            access to your library or your audio in the first place, so it cannot leak any of it.
            Mixar never collects:
          </p>
          <ul class="mt-3 list-disc space-y-2 pl-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">
            <li>Track titles, artists, albums, genres, or any other metadata.</li>
            <li>File names, file paths, or anything about your folders and collections.</li>
            <li>Your name, your email, your username, or your account, because there is no account.</li>
            <li>Your MIDI controller's device identifiers.</li>
            <li>Your audio, ever.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2 class="text-[1.5rem] font-bold tracking-tight">Turning it off</h2>
          <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-muted">
            In the app, switch off "Send anonymous usage data" in Settings. Doing so deletes the
            stored identifier and any queued events, so re-enabling later starts a fresh anonymous
            identity rather than reviving the old one.
          </p>
          <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-muted">
            On this website, enabling Do Not Track or using a content blocker that blocks
            <code class="rounded-sm bg-bg-elevated px-1 py-0.5 font-mono text-[0.875rem]">eu.i.posthog.com</code>
            stops all of it. Because the mode is cookieless, blocking it costs nothing: there is no
            stored state to fall back to.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2 class="text-[1.5rem] font-bold tracking-tight">Questions</h2>
          <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-muted">
            The analytics code is small and readable, and so is the rest of Mixar. Open an issue on
            the repository if anything here is unclear or looks wrong.
          </p>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
